The signaling pathway involves two enzymes:  
1. **Phosphatidylinositol 3-kinase (PI3K)**  
2. **Phosphoprotein B (Akt/ErB)**  

**Explanation:**  
The drugs mentioned (wortmannin, Ly294002, and BX795) are all inhibitors of PI3K. The fact that signal-induced transcription fails in their presence indicates that PI3K is a critical component of the signaling pathway.  

PI3K is a well-known kinase that phosphorylates phosphatidylinositol 3-phosphate (PI3P) to form phosphatidylinositol 3,4-bisphosphate (P13P). This P13P recruits and activates the protein kinase **Akt (also known as ErB)**. Akt is a key downstream target of PI3K and is involved in the activation of transcription factors that regulate gene expression.  

Thus, the signaling pathway involves PI3K phosphorylating P13P, which in turn activates Akt, which then activates transcription factors to induce the transcription of the target genes.  

\boxed{
\text{Cycine} \rightarrow \text{PI3K} \rightarrow \text{P13P} \rightarrow \text{Akt} \rightarrow \text{Transcription Factors} \rightarrow \text{Gene Transcription}
}

