The terms "isototopologues" and "isotopomers" are used to describe molecules with different isotopic compositions. However, they refer to different types of isotopic substitutions:  
1. **Isototopologues**: These are molecules that have the same molecular formula but differ in the isotopic composition of one or more atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) can be \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) (where all hydrogen atoms are \( ^1\text{H} \)) or \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) (where one hydrogen is \( ^2\text{H} \)).  
2. **Isotopomers**: These are molecules that have the same molecular formula and the same connectivity of atoms but differ in the isotopic composition of one or more atoms. For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) can be \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) (where all carbon atoms are \( ^1\text{C} \)) or \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) (where one carbon atom is \( ^2\text{C} \)).  

Thus, **isototopologues** are a broader term that includes **isotopomers** as a specific case.  
- Isotopomers are isotopologues where the isotopic substitution does not change the connectivity of atoms.  
- Isototopologues can include isotopic substitutions that change the connectivity of atoms.  

For example, \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) and \( \text{C}_6\text{H}_2\text{O}_3 \) are isotopologues but not isotopomers, as the isotopic substitution changes the connectivity of atoms.  

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Isototopologues: Same molecular formula, different isotopic composition (including connectivity changes)} \\
&\text{Isotopomers: Same molecular formula, same connectivity, different isotopic composition}
\end{aligned}
}

The reaction of toluene to benzaldehyde using chromyl chloride is known as the **Tollens' reaction** (or **Tollens' reagent oxidation**). This reaction involves the oxidation of the methyl group of toluene to an aldehyde group, forming benzaldehyde. The chromyl chloride (CrO(Cl)₂) acts as the oxidizing agent in this reaction. 

\[
\text{Toluene} + \text{CrO(Cl)}_2 \rightarrow \text{Benzaldehyde} + \text{CrCl}_2 + \text{HCl}
\]

\boxed{\text{Tollens' reaction}}

